justment": 2.23, "key_factors": ["Prediction markets price a Democratic flip at roughly 93 percent", "All ten expert ratings call the seat a Democratic flip, several at Likely D", "Bennett leads in fundraising and outside support despite Kean's cash-on-hand edge", "Kean weakened by ICE backlash, GOP defections, and editorial endorsements for Bennett"], "rationale": "An even district plus a strong Democratic environment already favored the challenger, and markets, unanimous flip ratings, Bennett's fundraising edge, and unfavorable coverage of Kean all point the same direction; the lone recent poll showing Bennett up five corroborates a Democratic lead.", "watch": "Fresh polling showing Kean closing the gap or consolidating Republican support would move the race back toward a toss-up.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Thomas Kean Jr. (R) is running."]}, "overview": "New Jersey's seventh district sits at even partisan lean, but first-term Republican Tom Kean Jr. faces a serious challenge from Democrat Rebecca Bennett. Polling has been sparse, though the most recent survey gave Bennett a five-point lead among likely voters. Prediction markets put Democratic odds near 93 percent, and every major race rating backs a flip, with several calling it Likely Democratic.\n\nBennett has out-raised Kean roughly eight million dollars to six million, though he holds a modest cash advantage, and outside groups have spent more to help her than to help him. Headlines describe backlash to immigration enforcement in the district, Republican defections from Kean, and newspaper endorsements for Bennett. The combination of a favorable national environment, headwinds facing an incumbent in a Democratic-leaning year, and Bennett's financial edge points to a Democratic margin of roughly eight to nine points, with meaningful residual uncertainty from thin polling.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.8072, "margin": 5.29}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.8017, "margin"